{"command": "python3 - <<'PYEOF'\np='/testbed/specs/001-transactions/spec.md'\ns=open(p).read()\nanchor='## Requirements *(mandatory)*'\nassert anchor in s\nedge='''- TTL on replacement: re-putting an existing key with `ttl=None` clears any\n  prior expiry (entry becomes immortal); re-putting with a finite TTL sets a\n  fresh absolute expiry computed from the current `timer()` value (the prior\n  expiry is never extended or reused).\n- `ttl=0`: the entry is expired at insertion time; it is never observable via\n  `get`, `len`, or invalidation, and it occupies no effective capacity in\n  observable state (it is purged before any operation).\n- Exact expiry boundary: at `timer() == timer_at_put + ttl` the entry is\n  expired (comparison is inclusive); just before that instant it is live.\n- Invalid TTL values: negative numbers, `bool` (`True`/`False`), non-numeric\n  types, and non-finite numbers (infinity, NaN) all raise `ValueError` before\n  any state change; `ttl=None` is always valid.\n- Expired entry with tags: its tag memberships are removed at purge time, so\n  later invalidation of that tag counts only live entries.\n- Purge ordering: expiry purging happens before the requested operation and\n  does not reorder or otherwise touch the recency of surviving entries; a\n  `get` that follows a purge still refreshes the hit entry\\'s recency as usual.\n- `timer` argument: any callable returning a number is accepted; the default\n  (`timer=None`) is the standard monotonic clock; the timer is consulted only\n  for expiry decisions and never mutates caller state.\n- Backward compatibility: caches and `put` calls that never use `timer`/`ttl`\n  behave exactly as before milestone 3 (no expiry, no clock reads required for\n  observable behavior).\n\n'''\ns=s.replace(anchor, edge+anchor, 1)\nopen(p,'w').write(s)\nprint('ok edges inserted')\nPYEOF", "exit_code": 0, "stderr": "", "stdout": "ok edges inserted\n"}